TY  - JOUR
AU  - Winston, Drew J.; Emmanouilides, Christos E.; Busuttil, Ronald W.
TI  - Infections in Liver Transplant Recipients
PY  - 1995
AB  - The first human orthotopic liver transplantation was performed by Dr. Thomas Starzl in 1963. Over the next 15-20 years, liver transplantation remained primarily a research procedure performed in a limited number of cases at just a few medical centers. However, after major improvements in surgical technique, organ preservation, immunosuppressive agents, and overall postoperative care led to improved rates of survival for transplant recipients, participants at a National Institutes of Health Consensus Conference in 1983 concluded that liver transplantation should no longer be regarded as a research tool but should be considered an effective therapeutic option for patients with end-stage liver disease [1]. Consequently, the number of indications for transplantation, the number of patients undergoing transplantation, and the number of centers performing transplantation have increased dramatically over the last 10 years. In 1993, more than 3,000 cadaveric livers were transplanted at more than 200 centers in the United States and other parts of the world [2]. Because of a current shortage of organs, a few centers are also transplanting partial liver segments or lobes from living related donors to children. Indications for liver transplantation include biliary atresia, childhood intrahepatic cholestatic diseases, metabolic disorders, chronic active hepatitis, primary biliary cirrhosis, autoimmune cirrhosis, primary sclerosing cholangitis, Budd-Chiari syndrome, fulminant hepatic failure, and (in selected cases) alcoholic cirrhosis and primary hepatocellular carcinoma. One-year survival rates for liver transplant recipients have progressively risen from 80% in the 1990s.
