TY  - JOUR
AU  - Rubin, Robert H.
TI  - Infection in the Organ Transplant Recipient
PY  - 1995
AB  - During the past 25 years, remarkable strides have been made in organ transplantation—the success of which can be regarded as one of the outstanding achievements of 20th century biomedical science. From a fascinating experiment in human immunobiology, organ transplantation has evolved into the best opportunity for rehabilitation for patients with end-stage disease of an increasing number of organs: today, the kidney, heart, liver, and lungs; tomorrow, the pancreas or pancreatic islets and small bowel (two organs being successfully transplanted in increasing numbers today, but not yet regarded as the standard of care for the diseases that affect these organs). In the United States at the present time, the 1-year graft and patient survival for these organs are as follows: cadaver donor kidney allograft, 77%, with 93% patient survival; living related kidney allograft, 90%, with 97% patient survival; heart allograft, 81%, with 82% patient survival; liver allograft, 64%, with 72% patient survival; single lung allograft, 52%, with 54% patient survival; heart-lung allograft, 53%, with 53% patient survival; and pancreas allograft (usually in association with a kidney allograft), 70%, with 89% patient survival (in the case of the liver and heart recipients, the fact that patient survival is higher than graft survival reflects success of re-transplantation for failing allografts in selected patients).
